Ceviri.ekle("en","ekokuma",{
"dorduncu-mustafa-sevilen-mi":{"baslik": ["Mustafa IV: The Historians' Common Verdict and the TDV Encyclopaedia's Objection", "c0acc696"], "metin": ["One question that comes to mind was this: is Mustafa IV a figure whom historians like, or has he drawn their thunderbolts upon himself? The answer is both: for a long time he was condemned almost unanimously, while in recent literature this condemnation has been openly challenged. I give both sides with their references.\n\nTHE TRADITIONAL verdict — harsh epithets. The TDV article `mustafa-iv` enumerates one by one the descriptions used by “almost all historians in general” and gives their sources: “weak in judgment and intelligence, unaware of the requirements of the age, so greedy for the throne and so merciless as to collaborate with those preparing a revolt that would endanger the survival of the state” (M. Cavid Baysun, İslâm Ansiklopedisi, VIII, 713); and “a partisan of the old order, feeble-minded, ignorant, greedy, short-sighted, a troublemaker, thoughtless, naive, unaware of the general condition of the state, a fool, one who did whatever the rebels wanted, one who thought of nothing but reigning” (Çelebizâde Âsım, Târih, II, 65; Mustafa Nûri Paşa, IV, 60; Ahmed Cevdet Paşa, Târih, VIII, 185; Mufassal Osmanlı Tarihi, V; Enver Ziya Karal, V, 84; İsmail Hami Danişmend, Kronoloji, IV, 88). The weight of this list matters: it is a chain whose links are not independent of one another and which rests largely on the same contemporary chronicles.\n\n🟢 the opposing view — the TDV article itself. Kemal Beydilli, the author of the article, says that it is “clear” that these epithets “do not accord with reality” and regards the judgment as “a matter in need of correction”. His grounds are these: ① Mustafa IV was deposed before he was given the opportunity to take any action that would show his real thinking about innovations — so no definite answer can be given to the question of whether he would have followed in his uncle's footsteps or taken a reformist path like his successor Mahmud II. ② The harsh measures he took against immorality in the city, without regard to whether the offenders were Janissaries. ③ His mentioning certain statesmen of his uncle's period, saying that if they were alive he could appoint one of them grand vizier at once, and his lamenting that no capable men were left around him. ④ His considering stationing trained soldiers in the Artillery Barracks at Taksim. ⑤ His taking a close interest, during his short reign, in the condition of the Engineering School (mühendishane), attempting to reorganize it and having a new, detailed code of regulations drawn up. ⑥ Relying on Zinkeisen (VII, 552-553): that in his reign the idea of reviving the Nizam-i Cedid was not entirely abandoned, that the sultan supported it covertly, and even that he charged Süleyman Agha, who had served as an instructor to the drilled troops at Levend Farm, with this matter.\n\n⚖️ the same article also carries the other pan of the scales. Beydilli also writes that Mustafa IV had belonged to the opposition against Selim since his years as a prince, and that those close to him, including his sister Esma Sultan, worked among the Janissaries for his accession. And there is one indisputable fact: it was he who on 28 July 1808 had the palace gates shut and ordered the killing of his cousin Selim III and his brother Mahmud; Selim was murdered, and Mahmud escaped wounded. It is also related that Mustafa attacked Mahmud in person (Mustafa Necib, Târihçe, p. 92) — this is an anecdotal report, whereas the order is not.\n\n📌 to the reader: the two sides are answering not about two different people but two different questions about the same person. On the question “What did he do?” the scale is heavily weighted, and the TDV article does not lighten it either. On the question “What would he have done?”, however, the sources are not sufficient, and the fact that one historian says openly, against all his colleagues, that “this judgment is in need of correction” shows that the second question is still open. An answer given without knowing which question you are asking does justice to neither side.", "4d70ed72"]},
"hukum-alani-mesafe":{"baslik": ["How Far Can a Capital Rule? Post Stations, Day Marches and the Campaign Season", "072f6104"], "metin": ["How far away can a capital really rule over territory? The Ottomans answered this question not in kilometers but in hours — and the records they left behind can be measured today.\n\n■ THE MENZIL: THE SPEED OF NEWS\nThe empire had set up halting points called menzil (post stations) along its roads. These were not evenly spaced; the source says so plainly: menzils were established at distances of “from three hours up to twenty-eight hours”, “according to geographical conditions, security and the places where the needs of the post station could be supplied”. In other words, from the outset the measure was not a flat distance but the difficulty of the terrain.\n\n■ HOW MANY KILOMETERS IS AN “HOUR”?\nWe have the daily register of the Uyvar campaign of 1663: each of the sixty-four halts the army passed from Davutpaşa to Buda is recorded with its hours. When measured with modern coordinates, all seven separate stages give the same band: one Ottoman hour is 4.3 to 5.4 kilometers as the crow flies. Three independent sources converge on the same figure.\n\n■ THE SAME WORD, TWO DIFFERENT SCALES\nAnd here is a distinction that escapes most readers. A KONAK is one day's march of the army: 19 kilometers on average. A MENZIL, by contrast, is the interval between the courier's stations: 85 kilometers on average. There is a four-and-a-half-fold difference between them, and the reason is simple — at the menzil the courier CHANGES HORSES and rides on; the army walks and encamps. These are two different speeds on the same road network. The place an order reaches and the place an army arrives are not the same.\n\n■ THE BOUNDARY DRAWN BY LAW\nWhat is interesting is that Islamic law, too, gave an answer to the same question. The distance required for a person to count as a “traveler” and be allowed to shorten his prayers has been estimated in all four schools of law at between 83 and 99 kilometers. In other words, in law, anything beyond this is already SOMEWHERE ELSE. This figure also agrees with the boundaries of the historical kazas (judicial districts under a qadi).\n\n■ NESTED RINGS\nWhen all this is put together, a nested order emerges: a fortress or village could truly rule up to about 40 kilometers around it, a kaza center up to 90 kilometers, a sanjak center up to 150 kilometers, and a provincial (eyalet) center up to 270 kilometers. Each tier is roughly the square root of three times the one before it — the very pattern that geographers describe in central place theory.\n\n■ AND THE REAL ANSWER: VIENNA WAS NOT TOO FAR\nThe campaign of 1663 set out from Davutpaşa on 18 March and reached Buda on 17 July: 121 days. But within those 121 days there had been only 258 hours of marching. That is, almost the whole campaign had been spent not in marching but in encamping, supplying and waiting. The army's net progress was 8.8 kilometers a day.\n\nThe campaign season — the period between the melting of the snow and its return — was roughly 240 days, and the outward march, the fighting and the return HAD TO FIT into that time. By this measure:\n\n   Going to Belgrade and back takes 184 days — comfortable.\n   Going to Buda and back takes 243 days — the whole season; no days are left for fighting.\n   Going to Vienna and back takes 290 days — it does NOT FIT into the season.\n\nThis is the answer to why the empire's northwestern frontier stopped exactly there. Vienna was not too far; it was too LATE. What drew the frontier was not a distance but a CALENDAR — and the same reckoning also places Baghdad, Algiers and Mecca among the places that could not be fitted into a single campaign. Those lands were administered by other methods, through governors, garrison corps and vassal principalities.\n\n■ THE LIMITS OF THE MEASUREMENT\nThe daily speed used here is derived from the register of a single campaign; it is not an exact figure but an ORDER OF MAGNITUDE. Even if it varied by thirty percent, Vienna would still fall outside the season — this does not change the conclusion, but one should not ascribe precision to the number.", "0770eac2"], "kisa": ["Vienna was not too far — it was too late. An empire's frontier was drawn not by distance but by the calendar.", "6d8e95c7"]},
"kabakci-isyani-niye-cikti":{"sebep.b": ["The incitement claiming that the yamaks (auxiliary garrison troops) in the Bosphorus forts were to be brought into the Nizam-i Cedid (New Order) system and made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms; the killing of Mahmud Raif Efendi, Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and Halil Haseki", "cf38e5a2"], "sonuc.b": ["Selim III's abolition of the Nizam-i Cedid with all its institutions and practices, his deposition and the accession of Mustafa IV", "4e31054c"], "bag": ["The visible face of the revolt was Kabakçı Mustafa, a Janissary from Kastamonu; the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam writes that the “real architects behind the scenes” of the uprising were Köse Musa Pasha, the deputy grand vizier (kaymakam), and the Shaykh al-Islam Topal Ataullah Mehmed Efendi.", "8f1c1945"], "metin": ["The setting. The Nizam-i Cedid had been launched after the Treaty of Jassy of 1792 and had carried out important reforms, but from 1805 onward it began to be shaken by reactions and acts of protest. The Janissaries and members of the ulema who were dissatisfied with its measures found an opportunity to widen the opposition front by taking a common stand. The attempt to apply the Nizam-i Cedid in Rumelia as well came to nothing because of the resistance of the ayan (provincial notables) and armed clashes (the Second Edirne Incident, 1806) — the TDV article `selim-iii` regards this retreat as the turning point of Selim III's reign. The closure of the pilgrimage routes owing to the Wahhabi revolts and the arrival of the British fleet off Istanbul in February 1807 dealt a heavy blow to the sultan's authority; the fleet's blockade caused scarcity and high prices in the city, bringing the accumulated anger to the point of explosion.\n\nTHE SPARK. Because of the war with Russia, the army had left Istanbul on 12 April 1807. When the forts on the Bosphorus had to be reinforced against the Russian threat, the intention of bringing the yamaks there into the Nizam-i Cedid system became an opportunity for Köse Musa Pasha's covert agitation. The incitement to the effect that the yamaks would be made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms, and that Mahmud Raif Efendi, Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and Halil Haseki, commander of the Hungarian battery, had been assigned to this task, resulted in the killing of these two men — this is the starting point of the revolt (25 May 1807).\n\nWHY it could not be suppressed. When the uprising became known in Istanbul, Köse Musa Pasha presented it as a mere outburst of indignation; the possibility of intervention by the drilled troops at Levent Farm and the initiative of the chief of the artillery were likewise eliminated by his schemes. Selim III was being given misleading information. The figure the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam underlines is this: the sultan, who failed to show the foresight to use the Nizam-i Cedid soldiers in Istanbul, who numbered more than 10,000, first sacrificed the officials demanded of him and then agreed to the abolition of the Nizam-i Cedid. Even this did not end the revolt; he himself was deposed, and on 21 Rabi al-Awwal 1222 / 29 May 1807 Mustafa IV was placed on the throne.\n\nTHE AFTERMATH. On 31 May 1807 a “hüccet-i şer'iyye” (a sharia court deed) was drawn up: in return for the soldiers' undertaking not to interfere in affairs of state from then on, they would not be called to account for the revolt. The deed did not hold. After the revolt Kabakçı Mustafa obtained the post of Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and fifteen days before Mahmud II was placed on the throne he was killed by Ali Agha, the ayan of Pınarhisar, sent by Alemdar Mustafa Pasha. The verdict of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam is severe: with this revolt the era of renewal was closed until 1826, and the state's “great opportunity to reform itself in a way that would keep pace with the developments of the age” was thwarted.", "58445285"]},
"kimdir-dorduncu-mustafa":{"baslik": ["Who Was Mustafa IV? A Reign That Lasted Fourteen Months", "b59dbb07"], "metin": ["Birth and years as a prince. He was born on 26 Sha'ban 1193 / 8 September 1779. His father was Abdülhamid I, his mother Ayşe Sineperver Sultan. He was brought up in the palace with a classical education; together with his brother Mahmud he accompanied his father on his tours of inspection in the city. Because his cousin Selim III was childless, he received affection from him and led a comfortable life as a prince.\n\nACCESSION. When Selim III, having put an end to the Nizam-i Cedid, was deposed at the close of the Kabakçı Mustafa revolt, he became sultan on 21 Rabi al-Awwal 1222 / 29 May 1807. He ratified the “hüccet-i şer'iyye” (sharia court deed) drawn up on 31 May 1807, stating that the rebels would not be called to account, and gave an assurance under oath. The document was of no use: those who had brought about the change of reign could not be prevented from interfering in affairs of state and forcing through whatever they wanted.\n\nTHE REIGN. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam describes these fourteen months as “such great turmoil, disorder and disintegration of the structure of the state that it would soon lead to regret over Selim's deposition”. The war with Russia that had begun in 1806 was continuing; the events in Istanbul spread to the front as well, the Janissaries revolted, the grand vizier was chased away, and the agha of the Janissaries, Pehlivan Agha, was killed by his own comrades. On 24 August 1807 a one-year armistice was signed at Slobozia, but this period was spent not in preparing for war but in settling internal scores. When, upon the resignation of the deputy grand vizier Köse Musa Pasha, Tayyar Mahmud Pasha, a mortal enemy of Selim III, was appointed in his place (November 1807), the Rusçuk Committee (Rusçuk yaranı), who wanted to put Selim back on the throne, gathered around Alemdar Mustafa Pasha.\n\nTHE FALL. Alemdar came to Istanbul with an army on 25 Jumada al-Ula 1223 / 19 July 1808; order was restored in the city, Kabakçı Mustafa was killed on his orders, and the Shaykh al-Islam Ataullah Mehmed Efendi was dismissed. When Alemdar stormed the Sublime Porte and headed for the palace to put Selim on the throne, Mustafa IV had the palace gates shut and ordered that his cousin Selim and his brother Mahmud be killed. Selim III was murdered; Mahmud escaped wounded and, as the last surviving member of the dynasty, was placed on the throne on 4 Jumada al-Akhira 1223 / 28 July 1808. It is related that Mustafa IV greeted the coup with the words “I have not stepped down from the throne; who has enthroned Mahmud?”, and that the sultan's imam Ahmed Kamil Efendi calmed him and saved him from Alemdar's wrath.\n\nDEATH. After being confined to his apartment in the palace, he did not refrain from sending word outside so that he might be restored to the throne. When about three months later Alemdar was attacked and killed and the rebels wished to march on the palace, he was strangled with the sash from his own waist on the order of his brother Mahmud II (28 Ramadan 1223 / 17 November 1808). His body was buried at Bahçekapı in the mausoleum of his father Abdülhamid I. Rumors spread among the rebels that the coffin was empty.\n\nFor the point on which historians differ regarding his personality, see the card `dorduncu-mustafa-sevilen-mi`.", "d41175c7"]},
"ruslara-niye-direnilemedi-1806-1812":{"baslik": ["Why could the Ottomans not hold out against the Russians in 1806-1812 — was it the army, technology, logistics or internal strife?", "25c658f9"], "ozet": ["In the summer of 1810 Sohum was lost, and in the autumn Rusçuk (Ruse). There is no one-sentence answer to the question \"Was the Russian army larger, or technically more advanced?\": the sources consulted point to at least five separate causes, and these feed one another. The card does not declare any of them to be the sole cause.", "3f5aaac8"], "metin": ["Cause ① — the throne changed hands twice while the war was going on. The war began in November 1806; in May 1807 the Kabakçı Revolt overthrew the sultan, in July 1808 a counter-coup overthrew the second sultan and brought in a third, and in November 1808 the grand vizier was killed. The TDV article `mustafa-iv` records how this was reflected at the front: \"The events in Istanbul spread here too and caused an upheaval; the Janissaries rebelled, the grand vizier was chased away, the army's senior officials fled, the agha of the Janissaries, Pehlivan Agha, who was handling these affairs, was killed by his own comrades, and Çelebi Mustafa Pasha was appointed grand vizier, but order could not be restored. The turmoil within the army led to a great weakness in the face of the Russians.\" Moreover, the time gained was spent not on the enemy but on internal score-settling: the one-year armistice made at Slobozia on 24 August 1807 was \"used not for taking the measures necessary to bring the war to a successful conclusion, but for the internal struggle caused by the change of sultan.\"\n\nCAUSE ② — the only modern army available was not committed to the war, and was then destroyed. Selim III had his New Order (Nizâm-ı Cedîd) army; although there were more than 10,000 trained soldiers in Istanbul in 1807, the sultan did not use them even against the revolt and abolished the corps with his own hand (TDV `kabakci-isyani`). A study examining the organisation of the campaign through the sharia court registers identifies the same gap: \"Considering that this war came on the eve of the New Order system introduced by Selim III, one would expect New Order soldiers to have taken part in it. However, Selim III did not use these soldiers in this war\" (Karakaya 2020, p. 116). As for its successor, the new Sekban corps (Sekbân-ı Cedîd) founded in 1808, it lived for three months: on 16 November its barracks were burned, its aghas were killed and the survivors scattered (TDV `sekban-i-cedid`). The TDV article `yeniceri` states the real structural deadlock in a single sentence: between the new and old armies a \"relentless enmity\" had developed, \"so much so that even in times of need it was never possible to use these two forces together.\"\n\nCAUSE ③ — who raised the soldiers: not the centre but the provinces. The manpower as measured by the same study: \"In the research carried out on the 1806-1812 Ottoman-Russian war, it is seen that the average number of soldiers remained around 60,000\", and at the front \"alongside the soldiers of the standing army (kapıkulu), it is seen that the troops of the ayan in particular were used in the war\" (Karakaya 2020, p. 116). The contribution of one Anatolian district (Safranbolu) is entered in the court registers in the form of demands for soldiers, avârız and imdâd-ı seferiyye taxes (extraordinary and campaign-aid levies), mules and sheep — that is, the army was assembled not through a central system of mobilisation but from soldiers and provisions gathered district by district. This is also the reason for the political weight of the ayan: the state depended on them for taxes, public order and soldiers (TDV `ayan`).\n\nCAUSE ④ — money. The revenue of the New Revenue Treasury (Îrâd-ı Cedîd Hazinesi), set up to finance the reform, had reached 64,500 purses in 1797-98; but because the growth of the new army \"increased the burden on the population of Anatolia and Rumelia, and the people were crushed under heavy taxes\", it fed the opposition, and when the New Order was abolished, \"one of the first things done was the plundering of the revenues transferred to the New Revenue Treasury\" (TDV `nizam-i-cedid`). In other words, in the middle of the war the army's source of finance was broken up.\n\nCAUSE ⑤ — several fronts and diplomacy at the same time. The war was not on a single front: the pilgrimage routes had been closed by the Wahhabi revolt, the British fleet had appeared before Istanbul in February 1807 (TDV `kabakci-isyani`), and in the Caucasus local powers had changed sides — in Abkhazia, Sefer Bey, the son of Keleş Bey and a Russian subject, wrote letters to the tsar in 1808 and 1809 stating that he wished to submit to him, and on 17 February 1810 the Russians proclaimed that Abkhazia had been joined to Russia (TDV `sohum`).\n\nAND the outcome can be read in two cities. Sohum Fortress \"fell into Russian hands on 11 July 1810 because sufficient military support could not be provided at that time\" (TDV `sohum`). Rusçuk, by contrast, \"withstood a long and bloody siege carried out by the Russian armies under the command of Count Kaminski; on 26 September 1810 it surrendered on condition that the garrison and the civilians could leave the city in safety\" (TDV `ruscuk`). Read together, the two do not yield the picture that \"Russian soldiers were better\": Rusçuk held out for months, and the following year the Russians were forced to withdraw \"after the heavy defeats and setbacks suffered in June 1811\". The issue, then, is not technical superiority in a single battle but the capacity to sustain a war: an uninterrupted supply of soldiers, money, command and political stability.\n\nWhat THIS card does not say. No quantified comparison of the Russian army with the Ottoman army of the period in terms of muskets, artillery, training and the education of officers was found in the sources consulted. The statement \"their weapons were better\" is too strong a claim to be written without being measured; the five causes above rest on measurable records, while the sixth (technical superiority) could not be measured.", "04d3cab8"], "bag": ["View: The cause was military — the old army was untrained and mutinous, and the new army was destroyed almost as soon as it was established. Counter-view: The cause was political — a state that went through two depositions, one killing of a ruler and an assassination of a grand vizier within six years could not possibly have maintained continuity at the front; the army was a result of this instability, not its cause. Third reading: both are secondary, and the real issue was financial and logistical capacity — soldiers were being gathered district by district, money was dissipated through the plundering of the reform treasury, and distant fortresses (Sohum) were falling because they could not get support. The sources present these three readings not as substitutes for one another but as intertwined.", "0b65dbbc"]},
"sebep-sonuc-amasya-1555":{"oneri": ["The Shah never gave battle", "670cbc9a"], "sebep.b": ["During the Nakhchivan Campaign, Shah Tahmasp's avoidance of a pitched battle and devastation of the army's route, Suleiman the Lawgiver's threat to Ardabil, held sacred by the Safavids, by wintering in Amasya, and the shah's request for a truce", "3fa28be9"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Amasya: the first treaty between the two states, in which the Safavids accepted Ottoman rule in Iraq and Eastern Anatolia", "dad38f4f"], "bag": ["Significance: The TDV describes this peace as the first treaty concluded between the Ottoman State and Iran. Although it had the appearance of a friendly exchange of letters, it made the other side accept Ottoman rule along the line stretching from Baghdad to Kars and kept the eastern frontier calm for a quarter of a century.", "338561c7"], "metin": ["The raids that the Safavids made into Eastern Anatolia after the Tabriz Campaign sent Suleiman the Lawgiver on campaign once again. Knowing that he could not withstand the great army, Tahmasp did not come out to face it; he burned the places the army was to pass through, had the water poisoned and moved the population away. Because of supply shortages and the approaching winter, the Ottoman army withdrew from Nakhchivan. While Suleiman the Lawgiver was in Erzurum, the shah's request for a truce was accepted (26 September 1554). The sultan's wintering in Amasya and his threat to destroy Ardabil alarmed Tahmasp: his envoy Ferruhzad Bey arrived in Amasya on 17 May 1555, was received in the divan on 22 May and presented the letter seeking peace.", "ac004014"]},
"sebep-sonuc-belgrad-1739":{"oneri": ["The 1739 Treaty of Belgrade — the recovery of lost Belgrade", "62d1d3de"], "sebep.b": ["Austria's defeat in the war of 1736–1739, which Russia and its ally Austria waged against the Ottomans on two fronts, and the operation carried out to retake Belgrade", "9da8e650"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Belgrade: Belgrade and Northern Serbia were recovered; Azov was to be demolished and become an area from which both sides would stay away", "3929618a"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, Austria's eastward expansion came to a halt and its frontier in the Balkans changed little over the following 140 years or so; Russia, for its part, was kept away from the Black Sea until the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca of 1774.", "951e406e"], "metin": ["The peace opened a long period of calm between the two empires. When Austria was hard pressed in the reign of Maria Theresa by the wars of succession and the wars with Prussia, the Ottomans chose not to take advantage of this weakness; they held aloof from the insistent offers of alliance made by King Frederick II of Prussia. Russia, having waged the war far more effectively than its ally, began to emerge as a serious rival to the Habsburgs in the Balkans. The price of the return to Belgrade was also heavy: according to the survey of 1741, 721 of the 1,546 villages in Northern Serbia had been abandoned. Belgrade Fortress was rebuilt and made the largest fortress in the country.", "76b0567f"]},
"sebep-sonuc-berlin-1878":{"oneri": ["Who undid San Stefano, and why did the Ottomans again leave the table with losses?", "ac172c9e"], "sebep.b": ["The objections of Britain, Austria and the Balkan states to the Greater Bulgaria that Russia had created at San Stefano", "d520e887"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Berlin: the division of Bulgaria into three, the independence of Serbia, Montenegro and Romania, Bosnia-Herzegovina handed over to Austrian occupation, and Kars, Ardahan and Batum ceded to Russia", "c00b2d63"], "bag": ["Significance: San Stefano was a treaty ending a war that concerned only two states; the Congress of Berlin made the question the common concern of Europe's great powers. According to TDV, the principle of Ottoman territorial integrity laid down in the 1856 Treaty of Paris was openly violated, and territory was given even to Iran and Greece, which had had nothing to do with the war. The congress, which was expected to secure a general peace, did not produce the desired result and became the source of later disputes.", "e1a4c38d"], "metin": ["San Stefano settled the Eastern Question solely in Russia's interest, without taking the European balance of power into account. Britain could not accept Russia's gaining so much influence over Ottoman territory, nor could Austria accept the birth of a large Bulgaria in the midst of its own Balkan designs. The new Balkan states were also discontented: Serbia wanted Bosnia-Herzegovina and part of Macedonia, and Romania, although it was to receive Dobruja, did not want to cede to Russia Bessarabia, where Romanians lived. When no European state would consent to Russia's reaching down to the Aegean and the Adriatic, Russia accepted the congress it had at first not wanted. Before the congress, Britain asked for Cyprus as a base in the Eastern Mediterranean against Russia and made it known that it would take the island by force if it were not given; the Sublime Porte, hoping for British support at the congress, handed over the island on 4 June 1878, and a supplementary convention dated 1 July stated that if Russia returned the places it had occupied in the east, Britain would withdraw, and that ownership belonged to the Ottomans. The congress met on 13 June under the presidency of Bismarck. The Ottoman delegation was headed by Field Marshal Mehmed Ali Pasha; with him were the Minister of Public Works, Karatodori Pasha, and the Ottoman ambassador in Berlin, Sadullah Bey. The Ottoman delegation expected the terms of San Stefano to be softened, but each state protected its own interest, and even the British representatives, from whom help was expected, expressed views against the Ottomans. The sixty-four-article treaty signed a month later also shaped the following years through articles not included on the provisions card: the application of the 1868 regulation in Crete, with Europe to be kept informed; the cession of Kotur to Iran; the closure of the Danube to warships while it remained open to merchant ships; reforms in the places inhabited by Armenians; and the retention of the status of the Straits laid down in the London treaty of 1841 and the Paris treaty of 1856.", "622cb3da"]},
"sebep-sonuc-bucas-1672":{"oneri": ["Why did the peace that won Podolia not last even a year?", "40c4c95f"], "sebep.b": ["Mehmed IV's Polish campaign: the capture of Kamianets (Kamaniçe) after a ten-day siege and the pressure put on Lviv (İlbav)", "c48f0660"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Buczacz: Podolia became an Ottoman province (eyalet), and Poland agreed to pay an annual pişkeş (tributary gift)", "74e7b1a2"], "bag": ["Significance: With Kamianets and Podolia, the Ottomans established themselves in a region regarded as the key to the Crimea; Ukraine under Doroshenko became an autonomous entity attached to the Ottomans, and the way to emigration was opened to the Lipka Tatars. But it was precisely this gain that was unacceptable to Poland: the Polish Diet found the treaty too harsh, rejected it and imposed new taxes. From 1673 the war began again and lasted until Karlowitz; the intervening treaties of 1676 and 1678 took Buczacz as their basis. Poland, unable to recover Kamianets by war, regained it only in 1699 through an article added at Karlowitz.", "affa5e12"], "metin": ["When Kamianets fell on 27 August 1672, the Ottoman side began to press Poland towards a lasting peace: on 4 September the Polish king was informed that the campaign would continue unless he accepted that Podolia belonged to the Ottomans, and on 6 September the army marched on Lviv, where the king was staying. On 19 September the king asked for peace; arguing that Lviv lay outside Podolia, he offered money in return for the lifting of the siege. When the army laid siege to the palanka of Buczacz on 3 October, the Polish envoys arrived and on 14–16 October held talks with a delegation headed by the kaymakam Kara Mustafa Pasha. The treaty was signed on 18 October; Fazıl Ahmed Pasha sealed the Turkish text and the Polish envoys the Latin one. At the envoys' request, the palanka of Buczacz, which lay outside Podolia, was returned to Poland, and that same evening a pardon (aman) for the Polish king was proclaimed by firman. However, Poland held on to some of the castles and palankas it had undertaken to return and turned to a hostile policy towards the Ottomans; the Ottoman government decided on a second campaign.", "8a5ad46f"]},
"sebep-sonuc-bukres-1812":{"oneri": ["What was saved at the table while Napoleon marched on Moscow?", "bd02bd79"], "sebep.b": ["The breakdown of the Ottoman-Russian alliance formed against the French when the Russians incited the Greeks of the Morea and the islands to revolt, and the war that resumed in 1806", "76aa0d95"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Bucharest: Bessarabia passed to Russia, and the Serbs were granted partial autonomy", "f12f7097"], "bag": ["Significance: With the Prut frontier, Bessarabia entered a period of Russian rule that was to last 106 years. According to the TDV, together with the autonomy granted to the Serbs, a period began in which the Orthodox and Slav peoples were openly incited and supported against Ottoman rule.", "33a756ee"], "metin": ["The autonomy provision was not carried out at once: the revolt of the Serbs, who wanted broader rights, was suppressed in 1813, Belgrade was retaken, and the leader of the revolt, Karađorđe, fled to Austria. But in 1815 a second Serbian uprising broke out under the leadership of Miloš Obrenović; although it too was suppressed the same year, from then on the privileges granted to the Serbs began to be broadened, and the question came back to the table with the Akkerman Convention of 1826.", "4aa66331"]},
"sebep-sonuc-edirne-1829":{"oneri": ["With the Russian army in Edirne, was there any room left to bargain?", "4b1ee7cf"], "sebep.b": ["The intervention of Britain, France and Russia in the Morean revolt of 1821, the sinking of the Ottoman fleet at Navarino and Russia's declaration of war in 1828", "deab6cdf"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Adrianople: the independence of Greece was accepted, and the mouth of the Danube and the Caucasian fortresses passed to Russia", "d1dd5d41"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, the Ottomans, unable to halt the Russian advance, could achieve no success in diplomacy either and were forced to accept the terms dictated by the victor; the payment of 11.5 million Hungarian gold pieces that they accepted was heavier than the treasury could bear.", "aeb7bf97"], "metin": ["On 22 August 1829 the Russian army entered Edirne without meeting resistance and also occupied Kırklareli and Lüleburgaz; while Russian ships attacked the Bosphorus, a squadron blockaded the Dardanelles. The Ottoman delegates who went to Edirne through Prussian mediation could not soften the Russian terms, apart from a small change in the commercial indemnity. The burden was later reduced: in 1830 the indemnity was lowered to 8 million Dutch gold pieces, and a further 1 million was written off on condition that the full independence of Greece be accepted; in January 1834 it was reduced to 5 million. The Russian withdrawal, which was tied to the indemnity, took place from Wallachia and Moldavia only in 1834, and from Silistra under the convention of 8 April 1836.", "6c6fcef5"]},
"sebep-sonuc-hunkar-iskelesi-1833":{"oneri": ["Why did a Russian fleet enter the Bosphorus while the Egyptian army was at Kütahya?", "64a31e5a"], "sebep.b": ["The forces of Mehmed Ali Pasha defeating the Ottoman army at Konya and threatening Istanbul; the acceptance of Russian help by the Sublime Porte, which found no support from France and Britain", "1dedc447"], "sonuc.b": ["The defensive alliance of Hünkâr İskelesi and the secret article providing for the closure of the Dardanelles in Russia's favour", "980eeac1"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, the secret article turned the Egyptian question into an international question of the Straits; it caused a crisis in Europe, gave rise through Metternich's mediation to the Münchengrätz Agreement (18 September 1833) and, when the eight-year term expired, was replaced by the London Straits Convention of 1841.", "c64e9a2b"], "metin": ["When İbrahim Pasha's army defeated Grand Vizier Mehmed Reşid Pasha at Konya on 21 December 1832 and reached Kütahya on 2 February 1833, both the capital and the dynasty came under threat. While calling on Russia for help, the Sublime Porte also wished to use this invitation as a card to spur France and Britain into action; Russia, however, gave it no opportunity: it anchored its fleet at Büyükdere (20 February) and then landed its ground forces at Beykoz (5 April). Through the Kütahya talks, the provinces of Egypt and all Syria, together with Adana, were left to Mehmed Ali's side (firman of 6 May). The Russian forces still did not withdraw; the alliance proposal brought by Orlov was signed on 8 July as the heavy bill for the help, and two days later the Russian fleet and ground forces left the Bosphorus. The TDV also sees behind the indignation felt in Istanbul at the Russian alliance the propaganda that Mehmed Ali Pasha conducted in Europe.", "f9d73540"]},
"sebep-sonuc-karlofca-1699":{"oneri": ["How did the side that lost the war manage to sit bolt upright at the negotiating table?", "35286fc6"], "sebep.b": ["The sixteen-year war on many fronts that began with the 1683 defeat at Vienna: Venice's advance northward from the Morea, the fall of Azov in 1696 and the defeat at Zenta in 1697", "b6bdcf0e"], "sonuc.b": ["Karlowitz: Hungary and Transylvania ceded to Austria, Podolia to Poland and the Morea to Venice; with Russia, only a truce", "f248d009"], "bag": ["Significance: TDV regards Karlowitz as a turning point in Ottoman history — for the first time the state accepted by treaty a loss of territory on such a scale, the peace was made at the table of mediating powers (England and the Netherlands), and the Ottoman-Austrian equality that had been accepted at Zsitvatorok but could not be put into practice was written anew. Even so, according to TDV the Ottoman delegation showed none of the abjectness of a defeated party: by holding on to Temeşvar and to fortresses in Bosnia such as Bihać, it preserved the possibility of recovering the lost places in the future. Ottoman statesmen saw the treaty more as a truce than as a lasting peace — an outlook that found expression in 1711 at the Pruth and then in the war for the Morea declared on Venice.", "c5337a74"], "metin": ["On the road to peace both sides were weary. On the Ottoman side, the army had withdrawn to the Danube after Zenta; Venice, advancing northward from the Morea, had taken Athens and many fortresses in Dalmatia and Bosnia; and the Russians had captured Azov in 1696. Grand Vizier Amcazade Hüseyin Pasha persuaded Mustafa II, who would consent to peace only once part of the losses had been recovered; his arguments were that the people were being crushed under extraordinary taxes, that villages were emptying, that finding soldiers and munitions had become impossible, that public order had broken down in Anatolia and that there were revolts in Rumelia. On the other side, Austria wanted peace because of the question of the Spanish succession, which was expected to erupt; Venice fell in with it, but Poland and Russia, not having obtained what they wanted, favoured continuing the war. The preliminary protocol signed at Edirne on 27 January 1698 accepted the principle of 'alâ hâlihî' (each side keeps what it holds). The talks that began at Karlowitz on 13 November 1698 ran to thirty-six sessions over seventy-two days in a large tent with four doors. The reisülküttab (chief secretary) Rami Mehmed Efendi studied the texts of earlier treaties and obtained reports from experts; even the Austrian delegate Marsigli wrote that the Christian delegates were at times reduced to a pitiable state. The longest wrangle was with Venice: agreement was reached when Austria declared that if the Ottoman demands were not accepted, Venice would carry on the war alone. Because the Russian delegate did not recognise the protocol, only a two-year truce was made with Russia; peace was signed in Istanbul on 14 July 1700. The terms were ill received in Vienna, Warsaw and Venice. The fact that the second delegate, Mavrokordato, later received a decoration from the emperor gave rise to accusations that he had acted against Ottoman interests; TDV says these accusations must be treated with caution.", "8373db96"]},
"sebep-sonuc-kasr-i-sirin-1639":{"oneri": ["How did three days of talks make a frontier for two centuries?", "e2bd586e"], "sebep.b": ["Murad IV's recapture of Baghdad, the withdrawal of the Safavid army to Qasr-e Shirin, and the exhaustion of both sides from the wars fought on the Azerbaijan and Iraq fronts since 1630", "4cf61a2e"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Qasr-e Shirin (Zuhab): Iraq remained with the Ottomans and Yerevan with the Safavids", "f48a09af"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, it put a definitive end to the Ottoman-Safavid dispute and was taken as the basis in later centuries: the Treaty of Kerden of 1746 renewed it, and the Erzurum treaties of 1823 and 1847 settled frontier questions on its basis.", "81507167"], "metin": ["The peace also brought economic returns. Shah Abbas's transfer of his capital from Tabriz to Isfahan and his founding of New Julfa for the Armenian merchants had shifted the trade routes southward; in the climate of peace, Iranian silk and textiles were carried via Baghdad to Aleppo, and via Mosul to Diyarbekir and the cities of Anatolia. According to the figures reported by the TDV, Iran's silk exports rose by about half between 1640 and 1670. This balanced period lasted until Nader Shah, founder of the Afshar dynasty, came to power in 1736.", "35eec015"]},
"sebep-sonuc-kucuk-kaynarca-1774":{"oneri": ["Why was a peace signed in a besieged army camp misread for a century?", "9811fc1f"], "sebep.b": ["The war of 1768, declared without preparation: the occupation of the Crimea and of Wallachia and Moldavia, the raid at Çeşme, the collapse of the Bucharest talks and, after the rout at Kozluca in 1774, the encirclement of the army headquarters at Shumen (Şumnu)", "757943f7"], "sonuc.b": ["Küçük Kaynarca: the political separation of the Crimean Khanate from the Ottomans, freedom for Russian ships in the Black Sea and the Mediterranean, and an indemnity of 15,000 purses", "f5c01ade"], "bag": ["Significance: According to TDV, this war both laid bare Ottoman weakness and testified to Russia's emergence as a great power. In the official French translation that Russia printed in 1775, the church to be built in Istanbul was rendered not as a 'Greek' (Rum) church but as a 'Russian' one; this falsification gave Russia a claim to a general protectorate over the Ottoman Orthodox and became one of the causes of the Crimean War. The claim was formally ended only by the Reform Edict of 1856 and the Treaty of Paris. On the other hand, Russia's recognition of the caliphal tie with the Crimea is counted as an important gain, even if it did not make up for the loss.", "b9b6b770"], "metin": ["The war was declared on account of Russia's interference in Poland's internal affairs and its attacks on the Crimean frontier — with just cause, but without any preparation. The Russians soon occupied the Crimea and Wallachia and Moldavia and crossed the Danube. In the Ottoman army, bad leadership, indiscipline, the collapsed menzil (post station) system and shortages of provisions heralded a general disintegration. The Russians were not at ease either; the Pugachev revolt that was shaking the country turned them towards peace talks — Frederick the Great had described this war as a 'war of the one-eyed against the blind'. In the talks held at Focșani and Bucharest in 1772-1773, granting independence to the Crimea was considered contrary to Islamic law, and the surrender of fortresses and an indemnity were also rejected amid the sharp reaction of the ulema in Istanbul; the war resumed in March 1773. In the spring of 1774 the Russians inflicted a heavy defeat on the Ottoman forces at Kozluca (on 25 June according to TDV) and besieged the headquarters at Shumen. Because even the interpreters had fled in the rout, Marshal Rumyantsev's peace letter went untranslated for several days. With the camp, reduced to 7-8,000 men, under siege, Rumyantsev insisted on negotiating while the war continued, without declaring a truce. The Ottoman delegates, Ahmed Resmi Efendi and Münib Efendi, held talks at Küçük Kaynarca on 16, 17 and 21 July; when the approval of the gravely ill grand vizier arrived, the treaty was signed on the evening of 21 July 1774. Catherine II found the terms more satisfactory than expected.", "494c3856"]},
"sebep-sonuc-mondros-1918":{"oneri": ["How did an armistice open the door to occupation?", "c3bb9cbb"], "sebep.b": ["The collapse of the Palestine–Syria front, and Bulgaria's signing of a ceasefire and withdrawal from the war, which left Thrace and Istanbul open to attack", "585fe0aa"], "sonuc.b": ["Armistice of Mudros: the disbanding of the army and navy, and the right of occupation given to the Entente powers by Articles 7 and 24", "26993965"], "bag": ["Significance: The TDV regards Mudros as the herald of the end of the Ottoman State's six-century history; the occupations that began with the abuse of Articles 7 and 24 gave rise to the War of Independence led by Mustafa Kemal Pasha and to the founding of the Republic of Turkey.", "6091cf80"], "metin": ["The British offensive that began on the Palestine–Syria front on 19 September 1918 routed the Ottoman forces; with Bulgaria's withdrawal on 29 September, the land link with the allies was cut. Two peace offers made by the Ottomans through Spain (5 and 12 October) went unanswered; the government of Talat Pasha, having received a vote of no confidence, was replaced by that of Ahmed İzzet Pasha (14 October), and Admiral Calthorpe was reached through the captive British General Townshend. Trusting in Wilson's principles, the government sent its delegation in the expectation that there would be no interference in the administration and no landing of foreign troops in the country; the text signed, however, dismantled the Ottoman defence system almost entirely. The British occupation of Mosul within a few days on the basis of Article 7 confirmed the suspicions; the Committee of Union and Progress dissolved itself on 1 November, and Enver, Cemal and Talat Pashas secretly left the country.", "3df03dd8"]},
"sebep-sonuc-paris-1856":{"oneri": ["Admission to Europe on paper", "a9a49644"], "sebep.b": ["Russia's demand for an internationally valid right of protection over the Orthodox population in the Ottoman Empire, the dispute over the 'holy places' and the Crimean War, which began in 1853", "e7a6090e"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Paris: the Ottomans were accepted as an equal member of the European states system, and the Black Sea was neutralised", "cbf265be"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, it ended the superiority that the treaties of Küçük Kaynarca, Akkerman and Adrianople had given Russia, changed the Straits regime of 1841 and was interpreted as having checked Russia's expansion towards the Ottomans, which had been under way since Catherine II. Because of the Reform Edict, it also drew the state into an important constitutional change.", "afbe2766"], "metin": ["The guarantees given soon ceased to work. When France was defeated by Prussia in 1870–1871, Russia announced, before that war had ended, that it would no longer abide by the Black Sea clause; the TDV regards this as the first sign of the great Ottoman-Russian war that was approaching. The guarantee of territorial integrity could not prevent the great dismemberment in the war of 1877–1878. The burden of debt left by the war and the social conflicts that arose in the implementation of the Reform Edict shaped the period that followed. The TDV reports that at the Congress of Berlin (1878) Bismarck rebuked the Ottoman delegates, who were trying to defend their rights, by telling them that European law was not for them — a sign of how far the status won in 1856 had remained on paper.", "71cfb416"]},
"sebep-sonuc-pasarofca-1718":{"oneri": ["The 1718 Treaty of Passarowitz — the Morea stayed, Belgrade was lost", "809d732f"], "sebep.b": ["The desire to avenge Karlowitz: the war declared on Venice; Austria's alliance with Venice in 1716 and its entry into the war; the defeats at Petrovaradin and Belgrade", "bddbeceb"], "sonuc.b": ["Passarowitz: the Morea remained Ottoman; the Banat, Oltenia (Lesser Wallachia), northern Serbia including Belgrade, and northern Bosnia passed to Austria", "c5eb1153"], "bag": ["Significance: According to TDV, the treaty served Austria more than Venice; with its great territorial losses the Ottoman state not only withdrew from Central Europe but also began to cease being a strong state in European eyes. The commercial treaty signed in the same days granted Austrian merchants free trade at a 3% customs duty and the right to maintain consuls and dragomans in all ports; in return the Ottomans too would be able to open consulates (şehbenderlik) in Austrian territory, and a consul (şehbender) was sent to Vienna. Belgrade and northern Serbia were recovered only by the 1739 Treaty of Belgrade.", "c503d80b"], "metin": ["Ottoman statesmen regarded Karlowitz as a truce. After halting Russia at the Pruth, they declared war on Venice, which they claimed had violated the treaty; the Ottoman army and navy had the upper hand in this war. Grand Vizier Damad Ali Pasha hoped Austria would remain neutral, but Prince Eugene favoured war, and on 13 April 1716 the Austrian-Venetian alliance was signed. In Istanbul some statesmen proposed placating Austria by returning a few unimportant islands to Venice; when the grand vizier threatened them with death they fell silent, and war was declared. Ali Pasha fell in battle at Petrovaradin, the Banat and Temeşvar passed to Austria, and in 1717 Belgrade fell as well. The question of peace set two poles against each other at court: at the head of the war party were Numan Pasha and the Köprülü family, and tradesmen who were profiting from high prices also wanted the war to go on; the people, however, were exhausted, and the sultan favoured peace. Charles VI's harsh terms, demanding Bosnia, Serbia and Wallachia, enraged Ahmed III, but through the mediation of the English and Dutch ambassadors it was agreed to negotiate on the basis of the existing situation. In the negotiations that began on 5 June 1718, Austria first made the surrender of Rákóczi and his men a condition; the Ottomans refused, and Austria backed down. In truth neither side was in a condition to fight: Austria was on the brink of a new conflict with Spain. The Ottomans asked for a fifteen-year peace; Austria's proposal of twenty-four years was accepted. After seventy days, the treaties with Austria and Venice were signed on 21 July 1718.", "ef9861d9"]},
"sebep-sonuc-sevr-1920":{"oneri": ["Debated for nineteen months at the partition table, forced on Istanbul for signature, never applied: what killed Sèvres…", "1720b20a"], "sebep.b": ["Paris Peace Conference: the victorious powers began to discuss the future of the Ottoman lands", "d3f8317e"], "sonuc.b": ["The Treaty of Sèvres was signed — the Ankara government did not recognise it, and the text could not be applied", "d11a8e42"], "bag": ["Significance: Sèvres was the written form of the solution Europe devised for the 'Eastern Question'. According to TDV, the 1923 Treaty of Lausanne preserved the distribution of the Arab mandates made at Sèvres, while introducing important changes in all matters concerning the new Turkish state.", "d797533f"], "metin": ["The decision-making body of the conference was the Council of Ten, composed of the prime ministers and foreign ministers of Britain, France, Italy, the USA and Japan; it determined the regions to be wholly separated from the Ottomans — Arabia, Syria, Mesopotamia, Palestine, Armenia and, on a British proposal, Kurdistan. The Allies could not agree: France and Italy wanted the secret wartime agreements to be implemented, whereas the USA wanted Ottoman rule to continue in regions with a Turkish majority, in accordance with Wilson's twelfth point. The Greeks, supported by Britain, France and the USA, landed at İzmir on 15 May 1919; this landing led to the rapid organisation of the national resistance in Anatolia. Faced with the demand of Indian Muslims that the caliph remain in Istanbul, the British government on 6 January 1920 rejected the plan to expel the Turks from Europe. On 16 March 1920 Istanbul was officially occupied; on 23 April the Grand National Assembly began its work in Ankara. The text was given its final form at San Remo (18-26 April 1920) and communicated to Tevfik Pasha on 11 May; Tevfik Pasha stated that he could not accept the terms. Lloyd George set the Greek army in motion on 22 June; after Bursa fell on 8 July and Thrace on 20 July, the Council of the Sultanate convened by the sultan on 22 July, in the face of reports that Istanbul would be occupied by the Greeks, voted in favour of signature, with the exception of Lieutenant General of Artillery Rıza Pasha. What followed came quickly: after Sakarya, France made an agreement with the Ankara government on 20 October 1921 and recognised the new state, and even Lloyd George declared on 10 August 1921 that Sèvres had now been torn up. The victory of 30 August 1922 was followed by the Armistice of Mudanya on 11 October, the abolition of the sultanate on 1 November and the Treaty of Lausanne on 24 July 1923.", "a078f4bf"]},
"sebep-sonuc-usi-1912":{"oneri": ["The last Ottoman province in Africa was given up at a table in Switzerland; but the war did not end there, and in the Aegean…", "4fa24612"], "sebep.b": ["Italy's declaration of war on the Ottoman State on the grounds of protecting its citizens in Tripoli", "8d96d53b"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Ouchy: the Ottoman State accepted that it had ceded Tripoli to Italy", "af6dab0c"], "bag": ["Significance: The loss of the Ottoman State's last territory on the African continent. The treaty's loose end was tied up at Lausanne: Rhodes and the Dodecanese, which Italy was supposed to evacuate under the Treaty of Ouchy, remained in its hands, and Turkey formally renounced its rights over Tripoli.", "b90736c2"], "metin": ["As the colonial race in Africa gathered pace in the second half of the 19th century, Italy's interest in Tripoli grew. According to TDV, Italy declared war in September 1911 on the pretext of protecting its citizens living in the city from the Ottoman government, and this was the beginning of the province's transformation into an Italian colony; during the occupation a significant part of the city's population perished. By the treaty signed in 1912 at Ouchy in Lausanne, Switzerland, the Ottoman State accepted that it had abandoned to Italy this last province of its in Africa; the caliph, however, would continue to be regarded as the religious leader of the Muslim community there. The treaty did not in fact end the conflict: Ottoman soldiers carried on the resistance together with the local people and fought against Italy under the name 'Africa Groups Command' until the Armistice of Mudros in 1918. Tripoli remained under Italian occupation until 1943, then came under British control, and independence was proclaimed in 1951. The other end of the treaty was in the Aegean: according to TDV's Lausanne article, Rhodes and the Dodecanese, which the Italians kept under occupation although they were obliged to return them under the Treaty of Ouchy, were left to them in 1923 by Article 15 of Lausanne; by Article 22, the rights over Tripoli were also renounced.", "a97d4df9"]},
"sebep-sonuc-vasvar-1664":{"oneri": ["Heavy losses at Saint Gotthard, gains at the table", "e178b653"], "sebep.b": ["Habsburg intervention in Transylvania, Zrínyi's building of a fortress opposite Kanizsa and Fazıl Ahmed Pasha's campaign of 1663, in which he took Uyvar", "c54bfafe"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Vasvár: the withdrawal of both sides from Transylvania, the retention of Uyvar and Novigrad by the Ottomans, and a twenty-year peace", "739b5091"], "bag": ["Significance: The TDV describes this treaty as the text that defined the widest Ottoman frontiers in Hungary. It also carries an innovation in legal terms: whereas at Zsitvatorok the peace rested on the sultan's personal pledge, Mehmed IV's ahidname of ratification also bound the rulers who would descend from him — the principle of the continuity of the state. But the peace alienated the Hungarian nobles; this discontent led to the Thököly revolt and, in the second half of the 1670s, to the two empires confronting each other again.", "b98a8982"], "metin": ["The crisis began in Transylvania: after the death of George II Rákóczi, the Ottomans took Várad on 27 August 1660, while the Transylvanian nobles consented to the stationing of German troops in some castles. In the talks held for two years with the Habsburg envoy in Istanbul, Simon Reniger, the 1662 draft of the reisülküttab (chief of the chancery) Şamizade Mehmed Efendi already contained most of the articles later signed; when Vienna dragged its feet, Fazıl Ahmed Pasha set out on campaign in 1663 and took Uyvar after a six-week siege. In 1664, after the unexpected attacks of Zrínyi and Hohenlohe in January and April, the allies besieged Kanizsa at the end of April; the siege could be raised only at the beginning of June, and at the end of June the Ottomans took and burned Zrínyi's Yenikale. On 1 August 1664, Ottoman units attempting to cross the River Rába suffered heavy losses against Montecuccoli's army (Saint Gotthard). This defeat strengthened the desire for peace at the Ottoman headquarters; eight days later the texts were exchanged in Fazıl Ahmed Pasha's tent. Leopold I delayed ratification in the hope of better terms, but because of the condition of his army and the Imperial Diet's reluctance for war, he ratified on 9 September; the confirmation took place with a ceremony near Uyvar on 27 October 1664. A note on the date: the treaty was signed on 9 August 1664; because the date in the Turkish text was wrongly converted, it appears in Western historiography as 10 August.", "598b1e60"]},
"sebep-sonuc-yas-1792":{"oneri": ["The army refused to fight", "d48bcd0d"], "sebep.b": ["Russia's annexation of the Crimea in 1783 and the Ottoman declaration of war on Russia with the aim of recovering the Crimea; Austria's entry into the war on Russia's side as well", "fa10a91f"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Jassy: the Dniester (Turla) became the frontier, and the hope of recovering the Crimea came to an end", "17bec341"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, the dream of recovering the Crimea was over, and with Russia's extension into Georgia the danger that would come from the Caucasus became apparent. The army's open declaration that it could not fight made the restructuring of the state in every field urgent; the peace was the starting point of the Nizam-i Cedid reforms.", "b7f66d88"], "metin": ["After the rout at Măcin (9 July 1791), the civil and military notables who gathered in the army camp acknowledged that the army no longer had the strength to fight and sealed a memorandum in favour of peace. The TDV describes this as a 'boycott', in the sense of the army's refusal to fight, without precedent in Ottoman history; Selim III, who wanted the war to continue, was left with no option but peace. Developments in Europe also played a part: Britain had opposed Ochakov (Özi) remaining with Russia with a firmness that went as far as the threat of war, and the danger that the French Revolution posed to monarchies had pushed Catherine II to end the Turkish war. The grand embassies sent by each side to the other after the peace were the last example of this tradition.", "1ecbc422"]},
"sebep-sonuc-zitvatorok-1606":{"oneri": ["Why did a title outweigh a fortress?", "95e3cb68"], "sebep.b": ["The financial straits into which the long war begun in 1593 had driven both sides; the Celali revolts in Anatolia, the Safavid counter-offensive in the east and the revolt of Prince Bocskay of Transylvania against the Habsburgs", "db194902"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Zsitvatorok: a peace in which the Habsburg ruler was regarded as the sultan's equal and the annual tribute was abolished", "b998d332"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, this was the first treaty in Habsburg-Ottoman relations to be decided by joint negotiation rather than depending on the sultan's unilateral goodwill; the two rulers dealt with each other as equal parties, and in diplomatic practice the Ottomans' superiority over their Christian rival came to an end.", "315f14fa"], "metin": ["Peace soundings had been made since 1596 in the winter months, outside the campaigning season, but had produced no result. In November 1604 the revolt of István Bocskay, Prince of Transylvania, against the Habsburgs interrupted the talks; the Hungarian nobles became the decisive party between the two great powers. Grand Vizier Lala Mehmed Pasha at first preferred to continue the war, but he died in June 1606 while preparing for the campaign. The treaty was later extended six times, and its terms remained in force for sixty years.", "2ac692fb"]},
"tabi-devlet-vassallik":{"sebep.b": ["The fact that the direct annexation of Serbia in 1439 and its conversion into a sanjak made distant lands beyond the Danube untenable in terms of the cost of defense and administration", "c33d4b54"], "sonuc.b": ["The recognition of Wallachia, in the Treaty of Edirne-Szeged, in the status of a tributary (vassal) state governed by its own voivode — the model for Wallachia, Moldavia, Transylvania and the Crimea over the following three centuries", "d4f4d91a"], "bag": ["According to the TDV article on Wallachia (Eflak), this treaty established 'a dual sovereignty': the voivode paid tribute and remained free in internal affairs, and the land was not divided into timars — the exact opposite of the solution applied to Serbia, which in the same period was directly annexed and made into a province.", "28922bc8"], "metin": ["A tributary (vassal) state was the form of government in which the Ottomans, without annexing the territory they had conquered, left the local dynasty at its head in return for tribute and troops. Wallachia, Moldavia, Transylvania, the Crimean Khanate and the Sharifate of the Hejaz were different forms of this model; what they had in common was that the land was not divided into timars (military fiefs) and that local administration and religion continued. Because it secured the same control more cheaply than direct provincial rule, it was preferred especially in frontier regions that were expensive to defend.", "3eed6869"]},
"tartisma-akdeniz-vehran-onemi":{"baslik": ["Oran (Vehrân): A Port Taken and Lost for Two Centuries — Why Was It Indispensable to the Regency of Algiers?", "b7f260c9"], "metin": ["① Geography itself defended the city. Oran was founded in 290 (903) by members of the Maghrawa tribe; its name means 'two lions' or 'lions' den' in the Tuareg language. The old city was built on both banks of the Ra's al-Ayn valley, and the 375-meter Aïdour hill to its west protected the harbor from westerly winds — in the words of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, 'its geographical structure has given the city a position that is sheltered against attacks from outside and strategic'. At a point very close to both Morocco and Spain, it was situated so as to control the sea route in the western Mediterranean. ② It was already a base in the Middle Ages. It suffered destruction repeatedly in the struggle between the Fatimids and the Andalusian Umayyads (297/910, 342/954), and passed successively into the hands of the Banu Ifran, the Zirids, the Almoravids (473/1080, Yusuf ibn Tashfin) and the Almohads; the Almohad ruler had a fleet built here and set out from here on his campaigns to take all of the Maghreb and al-Andalus. Under the Abd al-Wadids (Zayyanids) the city became the port of Tlemcen, the great trading center of the interior; for this reason the Marinids, who besieged Tlemcen in the 14th century, landed troops on the coast of Oran and had a fortress built here. When Granada fell to the Spaniards in 1492, Muslims and Jews from al-Andalus took refuge here and enlarged the city further. ③ 1509: the Spanish landing. On 17 May 1509 the Kingdom of Castile occupied Oran, killed most of its people and took the rest captive; no Muslims or Jews remained in the city. The Spaniards enlarged the walls and fortresses and turned it into a garrison town. ④ Five Ottoman attempts, four of them unsuccessful. Oruç Reis, master of Algiers, blockaded the city in 1516; but because the local rulers were uneasy at the Ottoman presence and cooperated with Spain, Oruç Reis and his brother İshak Reis were killed in 1518. Barbaros Hayreddin Pasha's attempts at recovery were not enough to retake the city either. In 1556, under Salih Pasha, beylerbey (governor-general) of Algiers, Piyale Pasha's expedition came to nothing because of the beylerbey's death. In 1563 Hasan Pasha besieged the city again but was forced to withdraw when help arrived from Spain. In the period up to 1708 the Spaniards had to contend with famine and plague as well as Berber attacks. ⑤ 1708: the first definitive Ottoman conquest — and its loss in 1732. After a long siege, the forces of Mustafa bin Yusuf Bey, who rebuilt Mascara, the center of the Western Beylik of Algiers, and who was popularly known as 'Bouchelaghem' (the big-mustached), received the surrender of Oran in 1708 in the name of the Ottomans; the news aroused great joy in Istanbul, and the bey moved his seat to the city. During the twenty-four years of Ottoman rule maritime trade revived. But in 1732 the city was retaken by Spain; with Spanish rule trade died down again and the population declined. ⑥ The earthquake of 1790 and 12 September 1792: the definitive conquest. When the great earthquake of 1790 devastated the town, the people founded a new settlement on the eastern side of the valley. On 12 September 1792 the Ottoman governor in Mascara, Kuloğlu Mehmed bin Osman, succeeded in finally retaking Oran from the Spaniards; this second conquest too was greeted with great satisfaction in Istanbul, and the people gave Mehmed Bey the title 'Mehmed (Muhammad) el-Kebir' (the Great). In total Spain held the city for 199 (1509-1708) + 60 (1732-1792) = 259 years; the 24-year Ottoman period in between falls outside this continuity. ⑦ Administrative and political importance: it became the capital of a beylik. Mehmed Bey made Oran the new center of the Western Beylik (one of the three sanjaks of the Regency of Algiers, the others being the Eastern/Constantine and Titteri/Médéa) — the TDV article on Algeria (Cezayir) records this as 'the West (center Mascara, Oran after 1792)'; moving the seat of the beylik from Mascara to Oran made the city no longer merely a port but an administrative capital. Mehmed Bey, who had the city rebuilt, settled the Muslim and Jewish population of the surrounding area here, restored to their original use the mosques that had been converted into churches, and built the mosques of Mustafa Pasha and of Mehmed Bey (Jami al-Basha), as well as a palace. ⑧ Economic importance: the gateway to Tlemcen, then the center of European trade. In the Ottoman period the city returned to its position as 'the port of Tlemcen', and maritime trade revived. After the French occupation (4 January 1831) it changed identity: while the Muslim population migrated to the interior, tens of thousands of French and other Europeans settled in the city, and Oran became the most important center of trade with Europe in Algeria. Today, too, its port competes with that of the capital, Algiers; according to 2009 data the population of the city is 852,000, and that of the province of the same name is 1,453,158.", "29bbef91"], "kisa": ["The city changed hands three times from 1509 to 1792 — behind Spain's persistence, amounting to 259 years in total, and the five separate campaigns the Ottomans undertook to win it back lay the same reason: the western gateway of the Maghreb.", "2f34ebac"], "bag": ["Its importance: because of its position, Oran was a gateway that was both easy to defend and valuable to seize — a port close to Morocco and Spain at the same time, controlling the sea route of the western Mediterranean and the trade of Tlemcen. That is why Spain held it at great expense for two and a half centuries, and the Ottomans tried to retake it in five separate campaigns. The definitive conquest of 1792 raised the city from a mere port to the capital of a beylik; only then was the coastline of the Regency of Algiers completed from end to end — and thirty-eight years later it became one of the first landing points of the French.", "aa3d7b1c"]},
"tartisma-kucuk-kaynarca-rivayetleri":{"baslik": ["Three stories told about Küçük Kaynarca — and the answer of the sources", "854b770e"], "metin": ["① 'The comfort of an elbow': It is told that the second delegate, İbrahim Münib Efendi, dozed off during the talks leaning on his elbow; that on waking and seeing everyone looking at him, he said, as though he had been following the discussion, 'let us come to the matter of the indemnity'; and that he thereby cost the state 4.5 million rubles in an indemnity the Russians had already given up. According to TDV, this has no basis beyond being an invented tale: the Russians never gave up the indemnity and had demanded 50,000 purses at Bucharest; the grand vizier had authorised the chief plenipotentiary to accept a demand of up to 20,000 purses without prolonging the matter and, if necessary, to go up to 40,000 purses. Since the indemnity was a separate article left to the very end, it was natural that it should be raised last.\n\n② 'Revenge for the Pruth': It is asserted that although the treaty was ready on 17 July, the Russians held it back until 21 July, the day on which the Treaty of the Pruth had been signed (Hammer, Zinkeisen, Jorga, Danişmend). According to TDV, the grand vizier's approval arrived on 21 July and the treaty was signed the same day; moreover, the Treaty of the Pruth too had been signed not on 21 but on 22 July and exchanged on the 23rd.\n\n③ 'In memory of Weissmann': The claim that the peace was made at Küçük Kaynarca in memory of the Russian general Weissmann, who had died there a year earlier, also appears only in Hammer; according to TDV, Rumyantsev's route and the fact that the peace could not have been known for certain in advance make such an intention implausible.\n\nNor is the account true that the treaty was 'dictated in a brief meeting of four to seven hours': talks were held for hours on three separate days, and the understandings previously reached at Bucharest were taken as the basis.", "71d21aab"], "kisa": ["A dozing delegate who brought up the indemnity, a date awaited to avenge the Pruth, a village chosen in memory of a general… TDV finds all three unfounded.", "51583f1f"], "not": ["The date of the treaty is also given differently in the sources: archival copies take 16 July, when the talks began, some primary sources take 17 July, and the Russian side takes 26 July, when the texts were exchanged. The date of signature is 21 July 1774.", "a379beab"], "bag": ["All three stories serve the same function: attributing a heavy defeat to an individual's lapse or to the enemy's symbolic calculation. TDV's objection, by contrast, rests on archival documents and contemporary Ottoman sources (Enveri, Vasıf, Ahmed Resmi).", "df4b9abe"]},
"yeniceri-ocagi-kurulusu":{"sebep.b": ["The need of the principality's army, from Orhan Gazi onward, with the crossing into Rumelia and the growing conquests, for a standing, salaried infantry force tied to the center", "cfd3ee6b"], "sonuc.b": ["With the accession of Murad I, the establishment of the pençik system and of the Novice Corps (Acemi Ocağı) at Gallipoli — the nucleus of the Janissary organization", "88edaafc"], "bag": ["According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the idea of taking one-fifth (pençik) of the prisoners of war, training them and adding them to the standing army matured gradually between the end of Orhan's reign and the time of Murad I; historians still debate the exact moment of foundation, and some point to the reign of Bayezid I the Thunderbolt.", "6ddd1be6"], "metin": ["The Janissaries were the infantry branch of the kapıkulu (the sultan's salaried household troops): youths recruited through the devshirme and pençik systems were trained in the Novice Corps and then enrolled in companies called 'orta'. The number of ortas reached 165 in the reign of Suleiman the Lawgiver and later 196; the corps was organized with the ranks of çorbacı and agha and was an institution receiving a regular salary (ulufe) from the center. Mahmud II abolished the corps by turning cannon on it in the Auspicious Incident (Vak'a-i Hayriyye) of 15-16 June 1826.", "b53f43ad"]},
"yenilesme-karsiti-ayaklanmalar-etiket":{"baslik": ["“Uprisings of Those Who Did Not Want Innovation” — What the Sources Say in Five Cases", "ff766e99"], "metin": ["In Ottoman history the uprisings that broke out right after a move toward modernization form a series, and they are often told with a single formula: a rising by those opposed to innovation. Below, five cases are listed with their reasons as far as the source states them; let the reader weigh for himself or herself how far the formula holds in each case. (The scope of this card is internal to the Ottoman state; for a world comparison see the card `kiyas-yenilige-tepki-ayaklanmalari`, and for a typology of the depositions of sultans the card `tartisma-osmanli-darbeleri-tipoloji` — neither has been repeated here.)\n\n① 1622 · The Incident of Young Osman (Osman II). The revolt began on 8 Rajab 1031 / 19 May 1622, and the sultan was strangled at Yedikule the next day. The trigger was the preparation the sultan was making to go on pilgrimage: a rumor spread that this preparation was a plan to gather troops from Anatolia and do away with the Janissaries. Here the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam enters an important caveat: the reports that the sultan had given up on Istanbul, that he would march against the Janissaries with troops from Damascus and Egypt and that he would move the capital are “the propaganda of those opposed to him, which is reflected even in the sources of the period”; the account of Hüseyin Tuği, who narrated the event through the eyes of the kapıkulu troops hostile to Osman II, passed into later chronicles and led modern historiography to regard him as a ruler who was “a reformer, an innovator, wishing to carry out wide-ranging reforms”. In other words, both the portrait of the “innovative sultan” and that of a “revolt against innovation” may have been fed by the same hostile source. And the reason for the ulema's resentment was not reform but the cutting off of their arpalık (stipendiary revenue assignments).\n\n② 1730 · The Patrona Halil revolt. It began in Beyazıt on 12 Rabi al-Awwal 1143 / 25 September 1730; on the night of 1 October Damad Ibrahim Pasha was surrendered for execution, and on 2 October Ahmed III gave up the throne. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam's list of causes is long, and innovation is not at the head of it: a political, economic and social reaction going back to Passarowitz, the loss of Belgrade and Temeşvar, the failure suffered in Iran, the burden of the imdadiyye (emergency aid) taxes, the granting of mukataas (revenue farms) for life, the tax burden on the artisans' guilds, and the luxurious spending of the statesmen, verging on extravagance. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam states explicitly that activities such as the opening of the printing press, the translation committee, the libraries, tile-making, the paper and textile workshops and the fire brigade “did not present a situation of concern to the broad masses of the people”; what attracted attention was the pavilions in the French architectural style, the luxury and the dissipation. Moreover, the preparation of the revolt had been undertaken by the grand vizier's own son-in-law, the kaymakam Mustafa Pasha, who had his eye on office.\n\n③ 1807 · The Kabakçı revolt. This is the case in the series that best fits the formula of “opposition to modernization”: the spark is directly the Nizam-i Cedid — the bringing of the yamaks into this system and the incitement that they would be made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms (TDV `kabakci-isyani`). Yet even here the deputy grand vizier and the Shaykh al-Islam stand behind the scene of the revolt; for details see the card `kabakci-isyani-niye-cikti`.\n\n④ 1859 · The Kuleli Incident. A conspiratorial society that was broken up on 14 September 1859, through an informer, before it had even gone into action. The causes counted by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam are: the discontent that the Tanzimat measures produced in some quarters, the financial shock after the Crimean War, the reaction against European-style manners among the upper class, and unease over the rights granted to non-Muslims by the Reform Edict of 1856 — but at the same time soldiers and officials who “had not been brought to the positions and offices they wanted”. Here too the label is not single: Namık Kemal interpreted the Kuleli Incident as a movement for liberty and found that the secret trial of the accused by the council of ministers was contrary to the legal principles of the Rescript of Gülhane. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam also writes that the movement later influenced the Young Ottomans and the Young Turks.\n\n⑤ 1909 · The Thirty-First of March Incident. 31 March 1325 in the Rumi calendar / 13 April 1909; it lasted eleven days, the Action Army took control of the city on 24 April, and on 27 April Abdülhamid II was deposed. The soldiers of the 4th Rifle (Avcı) Battalion who mutinied rose with a demand for the sharia; among the demands the rebels conveyed to parliament was also the full application of the provisions of the sharia. But the list of causes counted by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam is far broader: excessive politicization in the army, the conflict between officers risen from the ranks and academy-trained officers and the purge of the former, the loss of privileges by the old cadres, a sweeping reorganization and reduction of the bureaucracy, the struggle for power between the Committee of Union and Progress and the Ahrar (Liberal) Party, the repressive attitude of the Unionists, and assassinations by unknown perpetrators. And the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam makes the label itself a matter of debate: it was the Unionists who applied the description “reactionary uprising”; according to the article, in both Unionist and Islamist literature this description “has been seen as an instrument of the desire to bring back the era of despotism”, and “in our recent history the reactionary aspect of the 31 March Incident has been brought to the fore as more important than the incident itself”, serving as historical evidence for later politics. Moreover, Islamist figures of the period such as Elmalılı Muhammed Hamdi, Manastırlı İsmail Hakkı and Mehmed Akif openly declared that what had happened was unacceptable from the standpoint of Islam and that constitutional government could not be given up.\n\n📌 three patterns that emerge from the five cases — not a verdict, but an observation:\n· None has a single cause. In every case grievances over pay, arpalık, office and taxes are found within the same crowd as the complaint about innovation; it is often impossible to separate the two.\n· The crowd's reasons and the plotters' reasons may not be the same: in 1730 the grand vizier's son-in-law, in 1807 the deputy grand vizier and the Shaykh al-Islam, and in 1909 party struggle stand behind the scene.\n· It is most often the victor who has applied the label. The descriptions “reaction” in 1909 and “revolt against a reforming sultan” in 1622 come from sources that were themselves parties to the conflict. This does not mean that the uprisings were not opposed to innovation; it means that the description and the reasons must be read separately.", "5d0d6e5c"]},
"zimmi-cizye-millet-duzeni":{"sebep.b": ["The fact that with the conquest of Istanbul the empire's most populous non-Muslim communities became subjects in a single center, and the need for an institutional framework to administer this population", "e3883d25"], "sonuc.b": ["The abolition of the jizya by the Reform Edict of 1856 (Islahat Fermanı) and the imposition of the obligation of military service on non-Muslims — the legal end of the four-century-old dhimma order", "117c6dd5"], "bag": ["The TDV article on the dhimmi (Zimmî) states that the classical order was “to a significant extent abolished after the Tanzimat”; the article on the jizya (Cizye) records that the Reform Edict of 1856 abolished the jizya and that it was replaced by the military exemption tax (bedel-i askerî), which lasted until 1907. According to the article on the millet, the Armenian millet had been established in 1461 when Mehmed the Conqueror appointed a patriarch in Istanbul; after 1856 lay elements entered communal administration and the powers of the patriarchates were reduced.", "9ca5302e"], "metin": ["A dhimmi is a non-Muslim who lives as a citizen in the abode of Islam and has been given a guarantee of protection: his life and property are inviolable, his worship is free, and he has the right to take disputes within his own community to his own court; in return he recognizes the sovereignty of the state and pays the jizya. The jizya was levied only on free, adult males who were able to work and able to pay — women, children, the elderly, the blind, the disabled, the poor and clergy who had withdrawn to a life of worship were exempt — and in the Ottoman state it was divided into three classes: âlâ, the highest (48 dirhams), evsat, the middle (24), and ednâ, the lowest (12). The communities were governed through the millet organization; this organization was based not on ethnic or linguistic affiliation but on religion and denomination, which is why Gregorian, Catholic and Protestant Armenians were counted as three separate millets. The patriarch and the chief rabbi collected taxes on behalf of the state, governed the community, applied the law and supervised education; but this authority was under the control of the state — in the 17th century alone the Greek Orthodox patriarch was changed fifty-eight times.", "4b065364"]}
});
